Contents of https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/whats-new/dotnet-11/libraries:
This article describes new features in the .NET libraries for .NET 11. It was last updated for Preview 5.

## Diagnostics and process execution

*   [Process API expansion](#process-api-expansion)
*   [Console FORCE\_COLOR support](#console-force_color-support)

### Process API expansion

[Process](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.diagnostics.process) has a substantial set of new APIs that cover common scenarios where you previously had to wire up [OutputDataReceived](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.diagnostics.process.outputdatareceived#system-diagnostics-process-outputdatareceived)/[ErrorDataReceived](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.diagnostics.process.errordatareceived#system-diagnostics-process-errordatareceived) events manually or use P/Invoke.

#### Run-and-capture helpers

New one-shot APIs let you launch a process and get its result without manual setup:

```
// One-shot capture: stdout and stderr together, plus exit code.
ProcessTextOutput result = await Process.RunAndCaptureTextAsync(
    "git", ["status", "--porcelain"]);

Console.WriteLine(result.StandardOutput);
Console.WriteLine($"Exit code: {result.ExitStatus.ExitCode}");
```

The full set of helpers includes:

*   [Process.Run](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.diagnostics.process.run) and [Process.RunAsync](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.diagnostics.process.runasync) — launch a process and return an exit-status result.
*   [Process.RunAndCaptureText](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.diagnostics.process.runandcapturetext) and [Process.RunAndCaptureTextAsync](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.diagnostics.process.runandcapturetextasync) — launch and capture stdout/stderr together with exit code.
*   [Process.ReadAllText(Nullable<TimeSpan>)](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.diagnostics.process.readalltext#system-diagnostics-process-readalltext\(system-nullable\(\(system-timespan\)\)\)), [Process.ReadAllBytes(Nullable<TimeSpan>)](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.diagnostics.process.readallbytes#system-diagnostics-process-readallbytes\(system-nullable\(\(system-timespan\)\)\)), and their async variants — read a child process's standard output in a single call.
*   [Process.ReadAllLines(Nullable<TimeSpan>)](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.diagnostics.process.readalllines#system-diagnostics-process-readalllines\(system-nullable\(\(system-timespan\)\)\)) — synchronously reads all lines of output from a child process, returning a sequence of [ProcessOutputLine](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.diagnostics.processoutputline) values that distinguish stdout from stderr.
*   [Process.ReadAllLinesAsync(CancellationToken)](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.diagnostics.process.readalllinesasync#system-diagnostics-process-readalllinesasync\(system-threading-cancellationtoken\)) — returns a stream of [ProcessOutputLine](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.diagnostics.processoutputline) values that distinguish stdout from stderr without string parsing.

#### Fire-and-forget launches

*   [Process.StartAndForget](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.diagnostics.process.startandforget) — starts a child process when you don't intend to wait for it; the runtime detaches the handle automatically.
*   [ProcessStartInfo.StartDetached](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.diagnostics.processstartinfo.startdetached#system-diagnostics-processstartinfo-startdetached) — detaches from the parent's session/console so the child can outlive a terminal exit.
*   [ProcessStartInfo.KillOnParentExit](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.diagnostics.processstartinfo.killonparentexit#system-diagnostics-processstartinfo-killonparentexit) (Windows only) — the child is terminated when the parent process exits.

#### SafeProcessHandle lifecycle methods

[SafeProcessHandle](/en-us/dotnet/api/microsoft.win32.safehandles.safeprocesshandle) gains lifecycle methods for advanced scenarios:

*   [SafeProcessHandle.Start(ProcessStartInfo)](/en-us/dotnet/api/microsoft.win32.safehandles.safeprocesshandle.start#microsoft-win32-safehandles-safeprocesshandle-start\(system-diagnostics-processstartinfo\)) and a new [SafeProcessHandle.ProcessId](/en-us/dotnet/api/microsoft.win32.safehandles.safeprocesshandle.processid#microsoft-win32-safehandles-safeprocesshandle-processid) property — launch and identify processes without going through [Process](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.diagnostics.process) itself.
*   [SafeProcessHandle.Kill()](/en-us/dotnet/api/microsoft.win32.safehandles.safeprocesshandle.kill#microsoft-win32-safehandles-safeprocesshandle-kill) and [SafeProcessHandle.Signal(PosixSignal)](/en-us/dotnet/api/microsoft.win32.safehandles.safeprocesshandle.signal#microsoft-win32-safehandles-safeprocesshandle-signal\(system-runtime-interopservices-posixsignal\)) — terminate or signal a process by handle.
*   [SafeProcessHandle.WaitForExit()](/en-us/dotnet/api/microsoft.win32.safehandles.safeprocesshandle.waitforexit#microsoft-win32-safehandles-safeprocesshandle-waitforexit) and [SafeProcessHandle.WaitForExitAsync(CancellationToken)](/en-us/dotnet/api/microsoft.win32.safehandles.safeprocesshandle.waitforexitasync#microsoft-win32-safehandles-safeprocesshandle-waitforexitasync\(system-threading-cancellationtoken\)) — wait for a process to exit by handle.

#### Tighter handle control

*   [ProcessStartInfo.InheritedHandles](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.diagnostics.processstartinfo.inheritedhandles#system-diagnostics-processstartinfo-inheritedhandles) — specify exactly which OS handles a child process inherits, instead of using the all-or-nothing `UseShellExecute = false` default.
*   [ProcessStartInfo.StandardInputHandle](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.diagnostics.processstartinfo.standardinputhandle#system-diagnostics-processstartinfo-standardinputhandle), [ProcessStartInfo.StandardOutputHandle](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.diagnostics.processstartinfo.standardoutputhandle#system-diagnostics-processstartinfo-standardoutputhandle), and [ProcessStartInfo.StandardErrorHandle](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.diagnostics.processstartinfo.standarderrorhandle#system-diagnostics-processstartinfo-standarderrorhandle) — supply already-open [Microsoft.Win32.SafeHandles.SafeFileHandle](/en-us/dotnet/api/microsoft.win32.safehandles.safefilehandle) values for redirection without the framework opening new ones.

### Console FORCE\_COLOR support

.NET console output now honors the [`FORCE_COLOR`](https://force-color.org/) standard alongside the existing `NO_COLOR` support. When `FORCE_COLOR` is set, [Console.IsOutputRedirected](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.console.isoutputredirected#system-console-isoutputredirected) no longer suppresses ANSI escape codes. This is useful when you pipe `dotnet run` output through `tee`, into a CI log viewer, or through `less -R`:

```
FORCE_COLOR=1 dotnet run | tee build.log
```

## Text, serialization, and data handling

*   [String and character enhancements](#string-and-character-enhancements)
*   [Base64 encoding improvements](#base64-encoding-improvements)
*   [UTF validation and invalid-subsequence search](#utf-validation-and-invalid-subsequence-search)
*   [System.Text.Json improvements](#systemtextjson-improvements)
*   [Regular expression improvements](#regular-expression-improvements)

### String and character enhancements

.NET 11 introduces significant enhancements to string and character manipulation APIs, making it easier to work with Unicode characters and runes.

#### Rune support in String methods

The [String](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.string) class now includes methods that accept [Rune](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.text.rune) parameters, enabling you to search, replace, and manipulate strings using Unicode scalar values directly. These new methods include:

*   `Contains` - Check if a string contains a specific rune: [String.Contains(Rune)](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.string.contains#system-string-contains\(system-text-rune\)) and [String.Contains(Rune, StringComparison)](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.string.contains#system-string-contains\(system-text-rune-system-stringcomparison\)).
*   `StartsWith` and `EndsWith` - Check if a string starts or ends with a specific rune: [String.StartsWith(Rune)](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.string.startswith#system-string-startswith\(system-text-rune\)), [String.StartsWith(Rune, StringComparison)](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.string.startswith#system-string-startswith\(system-text-rune-system-stringcomparison\)), [String.EndsWith(Rune)](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.string.endswith#system-string-endswith\(system-text-rune\)), and [String.EndsWith(Rune, StringComparison)](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.string.endswith#system-string-endswith\(system-text-rune-system-stringcomparison\)).
*   `IndexOf` and `LastIndexOf` - Find the position of a rune in a string: [String.IndexOf(Rune)](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.string.indexof#system-string-indexof\(system-text-rune\)), [String.IndexOf(Rune, StringComparison)](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.string.indexof#system-string-indexof\(system-text-rune-system-stringcomparison\)), [String.LastIndexOf(Rune)](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.string.lastindexof#system-string-lastindexof\(system-text-rune\)), and [String.LastIndexOf(Rune, StringComparison)](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.string.lastindexof#system-string-lastindexof\(system-text-rune-system-stringcomparison\)).
*   `Replace` - Replace occurrences of one rune with another: [String.Replace(Rune, Rune)](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.string.replace#system-string-replace\(system-text-rune-system-text-rune\)).
*   `Split` - Split a string using a rune as the separator: [String.Split(Rune, StringSplitOptions)](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.string.split#system-string-split\(system-text-rune-system-stringsplitoptions\)) and [String.Split(Rune, Int32, StringSplitOptions)](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.string.split#system-string-split\(system-text-rune-system-int32-system-stringsplitoptions\)).
*   `Trim`, `TrimStart`, and `TrimEnd` - Trim runes from strings: [String.Trim(Rune)](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.string.trim#system-string-trim\(system-text-rune\)), [String.TrimStart(Rune)](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.string.trimstart#system-string-trimstart\(system-text-rune\)), and [String.TrimEnd(Rune)](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.string.trimend#system-string-trimend\(system-text-rune\)).

Many of these methods include overloads that accept a [StringComparison](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.stringcomparison) parameter for culture-aware comparisons.

#### Char.Equals with StringComparison

The [Char](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.char) struct now includes an [Char.Equals(Char, StringComparison)](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.char.equals#system-char-equals\(system-char-system-stringcomparison\)) method that accepts a [StringComparison](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.stringcomparison) parameter, allowing you to compare characters using culture-aware or ordinal comparisons.

#### Rune support in TextInfo

The [TextInfo](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.globalization.textinfo) class now provides [TextInfo.ToLower(Rune)](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.globalization.textinfo.tolower#system-globalization-textinfo-tolower\(system-text-rune\)) and [TextInfo.ToUpper(Rune)](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.globalization.textinfo.toupper#system-globalization-textinfo-toupper\(system-text-rune\)) methods that accept [Rune](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.text.rune) parameters, enabling you to perform case conversions on individual Unicode scalar values.

#### StringBuilder.MoveChunks

To transfer content without copying characters, use the new static [StringBuilder.MoveChunks(StringBuilder)](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.text.stringbuilder.movechunks#system-text-stringbuilder-movechunks\(system-text-stringbuilder\)) method, which moves all content from a source `StringBuilder` to a new `StringBuilder`. After the call, the source `StringBuilder` has a length of 0:

```
var source = new StringBuilder("Hello, ");
source.Append("World!");

// MoveChunks transfers all content from source to a new StringBuilder.
// After the call, source contains no characters.
StringBuilder dest = StringBuilder.MoveChunks(source);
Console.WriteLine(dest);          // Hello, World!
Console.WriteLine(source.Length); // 0
```

### Base64 encoding improvements

.NET 11 adds new APIs and overloads to the existing [Base64](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.buffers.text.base64) type, providing comprehensive support for Base64 encoding and decoding. These additions offer improved performance and flexibility compared to existing methods.

#### New Base64 APIs

The new APIs support encoding and decoding operations with various input and output formats:

*   **Encoding to chars**: [EncodeToChars](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.buffers.text.base64.encodetochars) and [EncodeToString](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.buffers.text.base64.encodetostring)
*   **Encoding to UTF-8**: [EncodeToUtf8](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.buffers.text.base64.encodetoutf8)
*   **Decoding from chars**: [DecodeFromChars](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.buffers.text.base64.decodefromchars)
*   **Decoding from UTF-8**: [DecodeFromUtf8](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.buffers.text.base64.decodefromutf8)

These methods provide both high-level convenience methods (that allocate and return arrays or strings) and low-level span-based methods (for zero-allocation scenarios).

### UTF validation and invalid-subsequence search

[System.Text.Unicode](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.text.unicode) has two new complementary features. [Utf16.IsValid(ReadOnlySpan<Char>)](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.text.unicode.utf16.isvalid#system-text-unicode-utf16-isvalid\(system-readonlyspan\(\(system-char\)\)\)) answers whether a sequence is well-formed UTF-16 without scanning twice, and [Utf8.IndexOfInvalidSubsequence(ReadOnlySpan<Byte>)](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.text.unicode.utf8.indexofinvalidsubsequence#system-text-unicode-utf8-indexofinvalidsubsequence\(system-readonlyspan\(\(system-byte\)\)\)) / [Utf16.IndexOfInvalidSubsequence(ReadOnlySpan<Char>)](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.text.unicode.utf16.indexofinvalidsubsequence#system-text-unicode-utf16-indexofinvalidsubsequence\(system-readonlyspan\(\(system-char\)\)\)) return the position of the first ill-formed code-unit sequence (or `-1` for valid input). Together, these methods let parsers, validators, and serializers report precise errors instead of generic encoding-error messages.

```
ReadOnlySpan<byte> bytes = [0xC3, 0x28]; // invalid UTF-8
int badIndex = Utf8.IndexOfInvalidSubsequence(bytes); // 0

ReadOnlySpan<char> chars = "valid \uD83D\uDC4D end"; // valid UTF-16 (👍 emoji)
bool ok = Utf16.IsValid(chars); // true
```

### System.Text.Json improvements

#### Generic type info retrieval

A common pattern when working with `System.Text.Json` type metadata is to retrieve a [JsonTypeInfo<T>](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.text.json.serialization.metadata.jsontypeinfo-1) from [JsonSerializerOptions](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.text.json.jsonserializeroptions). Previously, you had to manually downcast from the non-generic [GetTypeInfo(Type)](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.text.json.jsonserializeroptions.gettypeinfo#system-text-json-jsonserializeroptions-gettypeinfo\(system-type\)) method. New generic [JsonSerializerOptions.GetTypeInfo<T>()](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.text.json.jsonserializeroptions.gettypeinfo#system-text-json-jsonserializeroptions-gettypeinfo-1) and [JsonSerializerOptions.TryGetTypeInfo<T>(JsonTypeInfo<T>)](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.text.json.jsonserializeroptions.trygettypeinfo#system-text-json-jsonserializeroptions-trygettypeinfo-1\(system-text-json-serialization-metadata-jsontypeinfo\(\(-0\)\)@\)) methods return strongly typed metadata directly, eliminating the cast.

```
JsonSerializerOptions options = new(JsonSerializerDefaults.Web);
options.MakeReadOnly();

// Before: manual downcast required
JsonTypeInfo<MyRecord> info1 = (JsonTypeInfo<MyRecord>)options.GetTypeInfo(typeof(MyRecord));

// After: generic method returns the right type directly
JsonTypeInfo<MyRecord> info2 = options.GetTypeInfo<MyRecord>();

// TryGetTypeInfo variant for cases where the type may not be registered
if (options.TryGetTypeInfo<MyRecord>(out JsonTypeInfo<MyRecord>? typeInfo))
{
    // Use typeInfo
    _ = typeInfo;
}
```

This is particularly useful when working with source generation, NativeAOT, and polymorphic serialization scenarios where type metadata access is common.

#### Naming and ignore defaults

The naming and ignore options available in [System.Text.Json](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.text.json) now include:

*   **[JsonNamingPolicy.PascalCase](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.text.json.jsonnamingpolicy.pascalcase#system-text-json-jsonnamingpolicy-pascalcase)**: A new built-in naming policy that converts property names to PascalCase. It joins the existing camelCase, snake\_case, and kebab-case policies.
*   **Per-member naming policy**: The new [System.Text.Json.Serialization.JsonNamingPolicyAttribute](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.text.json.serialization.jsonnamingpolicyattribute) attribute lets you override the naming policy on individual properties or fields, giving you fine-grained control without a custom converter.
*   **Type-level ignore conditions**: Applying [System.Text.Json.Serialization.JsonIgnoreAttribute](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.text.json.serialization.jsonignoreattribute) at the class or struct level sets the default ignore behavior for all members, so you no longer need to repeat the attribute on every nullable property.

```
// Type-level JsonIgnore: all members use WhenWritingNull by default
// Per-member JsonNamingPolicy: EventName uses camelCase even though the
// serializer options use PascalCase
var options = new JsonSerializerOptions
{
    PropertyNamingPolicy = JsonNamingPolicy.PascalCase
};

var data = new EventData { EventName = "Launch", Notes = null };
string json = JsonSerializer.Serialize(data, options);
Console.WriteLine(json);
// {"eventName":"Launch"}  -- Notes omitted (null), EventName camel-cased
```

#### F# discriminated union support

The serializer now understands F# discriminated unions out of the box. Apps that share types between F# producers and C# consumers no longer need a custom converter for the most common shapes:

```
type Shape =
    | Circle of radius: float
    | Square of side: float

let json = System.Text.Json.JsonSerializer.Serialize(Circle 1.5)
// {"$type":"Circle","radius":1.5}
```

#### Utf8JsonWriter.Reset with options

[Reset](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.text.json.utf8jsonwriter.reset) now accepts a [JsonWriterOptions](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.text.json.jsonwriteroptions) parameter, so writer instances can be repooled with different options without allocating a new writer:

```
using var stream = new MemoryStream();
using var writer = new Utf8JsonWriter(stream, new JsonWriterOptions { Indented = true });
writer.WriteStartObject();
writer.WriteString("name", "example");
writer.WriteEndObject();
writer.Flush();

// Reset with different options for next use — no new allocation needed
stream.SetLength(0);
writer.Reset(stream, new JsonWriterOptions { Indented = false });
```

#### SerializeAsyncEnumerable improvements

[JsonSerializer.SerializeAsyncEnumerable](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.text.json.jsonserializer.serializeasyncenumerable) gains two new capabilities:

*   **`PipeWriter` target:** For pipeline-based I/O scenarios, new overloads accept a [PipeWriter](/en-us/dotnet/api/system.io.pipelines.pipewriter) as the output destination, making it easy to integrate JSON streaming directly.
*   **`topLevelValues` parameter:** For NDJSON output, set this parameter to `true` to write each item as a top-level JSON value separated by newlines instead of wrapping all items in a JSON array. Both `Stream` and `PipeWriter` overloads support the parameter.

```
static async IAsyncEnumerable<int> GenerateNumbers()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++)
    {
        yield return i;
        await Task.Yield();
    }
}

var pipe = new Pipe();

// Write a JSON array: [0,1,2,3,4]
await JsonSerializer.SerializeAsyncEnumerable(
    pipe.Writer,
    GenerateNumbers());

// Write NDJSON (one value per line): 0\n1\n2\n3\n4\n
await JsonSerializer.SerializeAsyncEnumerable(
    pipe.Writer,
    GenerateNumbers(),
    topLevelValues: true);
```

### 

